---

With the release of many LLMs from OpenAI and Anthropic, people who normally
don't write have started to *"write"* more. And by more, I mean a *lot* more.
Not that the quality is good, but the quantity (especially of words) is
definitely very noticeable. Trust me, I'd know. My boss and I use Claude at work
and I'm pretty confident that the one of the documentation he sends me is
definitely a medical-grade sleeping pill in disguise because it was sending me
directly to dream land within the first 30 seconds of me reading it. Either that
or it was a military-grade psychological weapon that makes your head hurts so much
you wish you were 6 feet under after reading the first few sentences. Some
people really do think quantity matters more than quantity.

On a related note, you've probably seen people (AI bros) talk about how they
teach their models to mimic their writing voice(?) or use skills to make them
not sound like AI slop. Posts like these don't really make me mad, but they do
make me wonder why these people only started writing after they have access to
these tools that essentially writes for them. My bet is on them wanting to make
money, and posting on platforms like Facebook or Twitter (currently known as X)
can make them a lot of money as long as there is enough volume of posts and
engagements.

Honestly, that's not the sad part for me. The really sad part for me is that
many people have decided that they should maximize the amount of money made and
minimize the amount of effort put into it. If teaching an LLM to *write like
you* is going to make you more money, then that is what they'll do. You
shouldn't have fun while making money anymore if there is a way to further
minimize your efforts.

I say this as if I'm immune to the hyper-optimized money-making mindset. But
truth be told, I know I'm part of the problem, too. I use Claude at work to
lessen the amount of actual work I have to do. It doesn't make me happy, but it
allows me to be lazy and still make money. It also makes me sad. _Really sad_.

No, I'm not being sarcastic. I still want to write code by hand. But having used
Claude for the past 6 months really allowed me to be lazier and decay my
mechanical programming skills to the point where I wonder that, if I went back to
writing code by hand again, will I still be able to keep up with the current
deadlines.

It's not a world I want to live in, but it's also a world I've walked myself into with my own two legs.

Obviously, this is not true for everyone. Inspiring people like [Andrew
Kelley](https://andrewkelley.me/) still doesn't use LLMs in his work on the [Zig
programming language](https://ziglang.org/) and does not allow LLM-generated
contributions to the language as well. People like him gives me hope that,
no matter how powerful and common LLMs become, there will always be people
like him who are still able to find a solid place in the world and can still
support themself doing things _their way_.

I liken this to manga artists who still use pen and paper to draw their
mangas even when drawing tablets exist. An example I know is Kuzumi Taiyou-sensei
who drew the Umamusume: Cinderella Gray manga that I love.
[Here is a video](https://youtu.be/BjA8WR9LAo0) of him doing the line work(?) of
a panel of the manga.

To me, someone who doesn't make manga, using a drawing tablet seems like the
more efficient way of making manga. The drawing step might be the same, but you
don't have to get your hands dirty from ink. You can use the digital tools to
help you with coloring or shading, and you don't have to risk spilling something on
the page and having to redo the entire thing. (You can still spill drinks on
your computer and have to redo due to your computer breaking but you get the point.)
You don't have to scan the paper into a digital file, and erasing is probably easier, too.

To me, it doesn't make much sense for someone to still be drawing on pen and
paper when a powerful tool like a drawing tablet exists. Why would anyone still
use pen and paper?

I don't have the answer to that, but I have a hypothesis: Because it's fun. I
also have another hypothesis about familiarity but it doesn't support my point
so we won't talk about it. I also think it's the more straightforward reason so
I shouldn't have to explain it.

Fun. What a lovely word. I wonder when was the last time I used it to describe something
that I did.

I think a lot of people, me included, have optimized the fun out of making
money. Why should you keep it? If you can make more money doing
something boringly, then you can use that extra money to spend it on something
fun later on. That should make up for it, right? The only one who can answer this is
you–the reader. For me, the answer is "not really." I want to have fun for the
most part of my waking hours, so I want the thing that makes me money to be fun,
too. Obviously, I'm speaking as someone who can choose. Not everyone can make
this choice for themselves due to their financial situation. But for those who
can, I'd love to push you towards optimizing for fun, too. Not everything in
life has to be hyper-optimized for the max amount of money in your bank account
if you're gonna feel like shit after the work day. That's your decision.
And I think it should be just that–your decision. If you're dead set on money
maximization, then I won't stop you. But if you're somewhere in between, then
I wanna tell you that it's OK to slow down to enjoy yourself more. Of course,
I'm just a 24 year-old right now so there's much that I don't know. However, I
know that slowing down and enjoying life more is something I will do. And maybe,
so will you. Just take this as a rambling of a stranger on his phone you might
walk past on the street.

Back to the title of this rambling, I still want to write with my own two hands.
That's why I've decided to spend some of my free time writing again on this blog
that I created on a whim. It won't contain anything life-changing or thought
provoking. It also won't contain ads or any tracker because I don't like that.
It will, however, exclusively contain the ramblings of a (born in 2002) year-old
who wish he had someone to talk to but decided to throw his thoughts onto the
internet instead of going out, touching grass, and making friends.

I hope you like it here. And if you don't, you can always just navigate away.
The internet is a vast place. You shouldn't waste your time somewhere you don't
like–somewhere you don't find fun.
